From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reelee81 <reelee81@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 18:28:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 17/18] pdf: Remember where inline images end to skip decoding
 them again.

An inline image has no length, so every run of a content stream decodes
each inline image completely just to find where its data ends, and then
throws the decoded pixmap away; an image that is drawn is decoded once
more. A page with 73 000 small CCITT inline images (pdf.js test
issue8078.pdf) spends most of every run on this, and a viewer that draws
a zoomed page in parts runs the same content many times.

The document now remembers, for each content stream (by its object
number, for an array of streams by all of their object numbers), the
offset and the length of every inline image it has decoded
successfully, when the image has no filter or only an image filter, so
its data is read directly from the content stream. When the same content
is run again, the remembered number of bytes is copied without
decoding; the stream position and the image are the same as before.
Images that fail to decode are not remembered. The list takes 8 bytes
per image and at most 4 MB per document. It is not used for an older
version of the document or while a local xref is active, and it is
dropped when an object or a stream is updated, the xref is repaired, an
edit is undone or redone, objects are renumbered for saving, and when
the document is dropped.

Modified for ReePDF on 2026-11-12.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
